se two types of links is a central theme [Data: Relationships (2079, 2119)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Challenges and Degradation in Wireless Links\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Wireless links ('Enlace Sem Fio') are susceptible to several factors that degrade their performance. 'Reducao Forca Sinal' (signal strength reduction) is a key issue, limiting the network's range and potentially leading to connection loss [Data: Entities (1588, 1599, 1545); Relationships (2114, 2135)]. This reduction is influenced by factors such as distance, absorption, and dispersion [Data: Entities (1580, 1581, 1582); Relationships (2108, 2109, 2110)]. Another significant challenge is 'Interferencia de Outras Fontes' (interference from other sources), where unwanted signals from other devices compete with the desired network signal, degrading communication quality [Data: Entities (1589, 1602, 1546); Relationships (2115, 2142, 2112, 2113)]. 'Ruido' (noise) is a general term for signal degradation, often caused by electromagnetic interference, which can corrupt data [Data: Entities (257, 1562); Relationships (2117, 2125, 2133)]. Furthermore, 'Propagacao Multivias' (multipath propagation), where radio signals travel multiple paths, causes interference and distortion in wireless links [Data: Entities (1590); Relationships (2116)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Key Technologies and Components for Wireless Networks\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The IEEE 802.11 standard, commonly known as Wi-Fi, is a primary specification for wireless local area networks (WLANs) [Data: Entities (1526); Relationships (2058, 2063, 2057)]. 'Infraestrutura de Rede' (Network Infrastructure) forms the backbone for these wireless networks, typically consisting of wired components like routers and switches, to which wireless devices connect [Data: Entities (1517, 1550); Relationships (2060, 2062, 2083)]. A 'Hospedeiro Sem Fio' (Wireless Host) refers to any end 